Two ascensions in a row, not bad. Obviously, my first (and totally
unexpected) one taught me a thing or two.

This one was quite generic, so I'll try to keep it short.

Being euphoric after ascending my abominable male Valkyrie I
decided to try the opposite - a Wizard.

Force Bolt and Magic Missile were my starting spells, and nothing
special among my rings and wands.

Basil, my beloved kitten, died to a rock trap on Dlvl1, but Dlvl2
had an altar, which made things somewhat easier. This also was the
*only* random altar in the entire game (no random temples, either),
but that wasn't too bad for a wizard. Dlvl2 and 3, a couple of
shops, couldn't rob them without a pet, but still picked up some
equipment. Made my way to the Minetown, visited Sokoban and
foolishly ate a couple of tengus in hopes of getting TC. Got
teleportitis instead. Soon after that blessed and quaffed my first
smoky potion, and got a wish. I had a strong urge to wish for a
SDSM, or a magic marker, or a bag of holding (Sokoban had AoR), but
in the end decided to wish for a ring of teleport control, and
never regretted it.

Only now my sacrificial fervor truly attracted the attention of
Anhur, which resulted in Magicbane, and that nice athame was used
exclusively for the rest of the game.

Visited Catacombs at the bottom of the Mines, killing some master
mind flayer on my way there. The thing wasn't too scary, so I
even thought about trying genoless.

Quest Home provided enough wraith corpses to get me to Clvl14.

The Quest itself was at times a little toughish but a few healings
and the E-word, - it was the only place in the game where I had to
use it, - fixed that. Dark One was a pushover, and fell to a finger
of death (the book was in one of the early shops, and I blessed it
to read it).

Eye of the Aethiopica made things much, *much* easier. From then on
I basically just fingered everything that tried being tough.

Got a wish from some throne room, and it went on a magic marker, I
think. Eventually peaceful gremlins started bothering me too much,
so there went my attempt at genoless. A reverse genocide for
wraiths didn't yield a single corpse, suprisingly.

Fort Ludios didn't exist in this game, either.

Medusa on Dlvl21 went smoothly, dying to my Sokoban amulet of
reflection.

Castle was annoying because of abundance of trolls and absence of a
tinning kit among my eqiupment. The wand was most conviently (0:3),
which allowed me to finalize my AK and write several genocides,
reverse genocides and enchant armours.

This time I explored Gehennom fully (good thing it only lasted till
Dlvl45). Juiblex gave me a little more trouble than last time,
sickening me twice and escaping. I somehow missed Asmodeus' death
message, so I spent some time searching for him, thinking he
could've escaped. Orcus and Baalzebub were absolutely non-descript.

I've found Giantslayer at Vlad's, and some other artifact was
wielded by some lesser demon, I think. I also had another
sacrificial orgy, which eventually resulted in Fire Brand and Frost
Brand, - but I never actually used those two, being thoroughly
happy with Magicbane.

I've found the vibrating square unexpectedly (I thought Gehennom
would last for another couple of levels at least). This was a
shock, as I hoped for some more loot (magic markers, especially,
to get rid of those annoying peaceful i's and O's). I even
resorted to some modest polypiling (a dozen or so unihorns), but
in vain.

So I sighed and prepared myself for the final rush. Rodney died to
a single finger, and though he tried to come back from the dead
five times, it was all the same: "Nyah nyah nyah! You thought you
could kill me, foolish mortal!!!" *zzzzzZZZZZZTTTttt* "Indeed I
did. Chalk up another one!" *collects loot from Rodney's deep fried
corpse*

So, after getting the Book, I levelported to Dlvl45 (c?oTeleport),
performed the invocation, and breezed through Moloch's Sanctum with
the help from my (random drop) ring of conflict.

The way back to surface was easy with my huge stash of scrolls and
wands of teleport. Planes weren't too much trouble with conflict,
but I forgot to bring extra tripe rations and suffered for that
after a couple of them refused to work. But anyway, the only
remotely troublesome plane was Air, and even there Air Elementals
were too busy with their brethren to give me much trouble.

Pestilence was in the Center, so I fingered my way in, and found a
lawful altar. Famine on the left, saw my finger, too, and the altar
was most conveniently chaotic. The end of story (and huge heaps of
bodies on Astral Plane from that incredible free-for-all battle).


